<header><p class="st-kicker">duchy · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Duchy of Saxe-Eisenberg</h1><p class="st-meta">1680 CE – 1707 CE · capital Eisenberg</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Tiny Ernestine duchy in eastern Thuringia whose single duke built a celebrated baroque palace chapel.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In the 1680 division of the inheritance of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ernest-i-duke-of-saxe-gotha">Ernest the Pious</a>, his fifth son Christian received Eisenberg and its district, between Gera and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jena">Jena</a>, founding the Duchy of Saxe-Eisenberg.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Christian rebuilt Christiansburg Palace in Eisenberg and added a richly decorated baroque chapel, still one of the finest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a>. He was interested in alchemy and kept a small Lutheran court. The duchy was largely rural, with no independent policy of note.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Christian died in 1707 without a male heir. After a dispute among his relatives, Saxe-Eisenberg passed to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-saxe-gotha">Saxe-Gotha-Altenburg</a>; in 1826 it went with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/altenburg">Altenburg</a> to the new <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-saxe-altenburg">Duchy of Saxe-Altenburg</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/duchy-of-saxe-eisenberg.jpg" alt="Duchy of Saxe-Eisenberg" width="320" height="280"><figcaption>Image: Sir Iain · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Locator_Duchy_of_Saxe-Eisenberg_(1680).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1680 CE – 1707 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>duchy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Eisenberg</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Christian</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Lutheranism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Sachsen-Eisenberg</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1694&amp;focus=duchy-of-saxe-eisenberg" aria-label="Duchy of Saxe-Eisenberg on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4951 410 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Duchy of Saxe-Eisenberg lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5271 666L5244 667"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5244 667h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5271 666h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5271 666h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Eisenberg. <a href="/atlas?year=1694&amp;focus=duchy-of-saxe-eisenberg">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
